Original repository path: docs/FOLDER_RECOVERY_VERIFICATION.md

# Folder ownership and save recovery

Recorded 2026-09-16. Implements another part of migration §5B/§8A. This is
workspace evidence, not production certification. The [execution ledger](MIGRATION_STATUS.md)
tracks the remaining product and release gates.

## Ownership and storage

One tab holds an exclusive Web Lock for each folder job. A second tab reads
the saved job without resetting discovery or processing and disables mutation
controls. It can acquire ownership after the first tab closes and the user
returns to it. Ownership has no expiring lease that could steal a suspended
tab's live job. Each acquisition increments a durable epoch with a random
owner token; claims also have distinct attempt IDs.

Workers hold a shared write gate through output validation, writing, file
closure and journal completion. Ownership handoff takes the same gate
exclusively before advancing the epoch. Writes for different images can run
concurrently while handoff waits for old writes. This uses the cross-context
shared/exclusive semantics and lifecycle described in the [Web Locks specification](https://www.w3.org/TR/web-locks/).

Every manifest mutation checks ownership against fresh IndexedDB state. Entry
completion also checks its attempt ID. Success/error delivery can repeat
without counting an image twice or changing a completed image to failed.
Status-only UI patches read fresh counters, and UI snapshots have increasing
revisions so out-of-order callbacks cannot roll back displayed totals.
Creation is insert-only. The existing database schema receives additive fields;
old completed entries remain completed when their jobs reopen.

The later [rendering-contract migration](FOLDER_RENDER_CONTRACT_VERIFICATION.md)
adds database version 2 with private font snapshots. Existing version-1 records
and outputs remain readable, but cannot resume with an unrecorded engine/font
identity. New jobs capture their recipe and fonts under an exclusive preparation
gate; ordinary output writes retain the shared gate described here.

## Output journal

Before file creation, a strict-durability IndexedDB transaction records the
intended path, encoded length, SHA-256, source SHA-256, dimensions and format.
The created file handle is saved before writing. Bytes stay in the admitted
worker and are written in 4 MiB chunks; only a closed output can be completed
in the manifest. A worker dying after close leaves an intent that a retry can
verify and count without rewriting the file. A worker dying during a write
leaves its owned empty placeholder eligible for retry. The scheduler reserves
additional output memory for journal readback and hashing.

The writer requests `mode: "exclusive"` and probes that a second writer really
fails, since older WebIDL implementations can ignore unknown options. A browser
that ignores the mode fails before writing. See [the browser API documentation](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FileSystemFileHandle/createWritable)
for the mode and close behavior. These locks do not control other native
applications editing files.

Already-existing unrelated files are refused. Journaled files with different
bytes are preserved and reported for attention. Sources changed since discovery
are detected by size/timestamp and require explicit Retry failed; once a save
intent exists, its source digest must also match. Source paths preserve actual
filenames and reject traversal rather than opening a sanitized substitute.
Output folders receive random suffixes to avoid same-second naming collisions.

## Executed evidence

`npm run verify:folder-recovery` uses actual Chromium IndexedDB, OPFS, Web Locks
and workers. No production fault-injection switches are introduced. It checks:

- Two-tab exclusion, automatic release on actual tab closure, increasing epochs,
  and rejection of stale claims, status changes, resets, completions and deletion.
- 24 unique concurrent claims and 24 real parallel file writes; exact counters
  after duplicate completions, late errors and UI status updates.
- Handoff waiting behind an outstanding worker write gate.
- Workers terminated during writing and after close but before completion;
  exact recovery bytes and unchanged modification time for a reconciled file.
- Retry-only-failed, obsolete attempt rejection, changed-source detection,
  preservation of unrelated and changed journaled files, and safe failure when
  a simulated older browser ignores exclusive-write mode.

The full UI suite passes against source and optimized Pages artifacts, including
second-tab disabled controls, ordinary folder transforms, pause/resume,
repaired-source retry and interrupted discovery recovery. An injected IndexedDB
quota failure checks that the UI retains a usable Resume action even if saving
the error state itself fails. The published Pillow JS/WASM pair remains unchanged.

## Boundaries still requiring qualification

These tests use browser-managed OPFS directories; actual user-selected folders,
permission revocation, operating-system disk-full errors, browser/process crashes
and power loss require the physical reliability matrix. Strict IndexedDB
durability plus successful file close does not establish a cross-storage
power-loss transaction. A process crash between creating a file and persisting
its handle can leave an empty placeholder that is conservatively refused for
manual review. File System Access has no portable atomic create-if-absent
operation; native applications can race browser checks. Do not describe this as
protection from arbitrary concurrent native filesystem edits.

Legacy interrupted outputs without a journal cannot be authenticated and will
be preserved for review. Reconciliation currently rerenders an image before
checking its existing output, so it saves a rewrite rather than all compute.
Same-size/same-timestamp source changes before the first digest are not detectable
from metadata-only discovery. Versioned source/engine/project identities and
full shared compositor integration remain in the migration scope.

Job ownership is per job and origin; it is not a browser-wide CPU budget across
different jobs/tabs. Large-corpus throughput, 100/1,000/10,000-image jobs,
sustained phone memory, all compositor/cache/model accounting, the failed engine
microbenchmark budget, and the complete production gates remain unproven.
